  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 AM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Some patchy dense fog is possible early this \nmorning, with an even better chance early Wednesday morning.\n\nWith an increase in low-level moisture and mainly calm winds as \nsurface high pressure extends SW into the area, some very patchy fog \nhas developed across parts of the NE Piedmont and northern Coastal \nPlain. This is the region where fog potential is greatest into early \nthis morning, and guidance shows there should be an increase in \ncoverage, with locally dense fog possible. Over the eastern \nSandhills and central/southern Coastal Plain, fog development should \nbe somewhat mitigated by an area of stratocumulus, while farther \nwest, drier air should limit development. Clearing will occur by mid-\nmorning, with a much warmer day on tap today (highs in the mid-70s \nto 80).\n\nAs the low-level flow turns more easterly and southeasterly, dew \npoints will be higher on Tuesday night. So expect more widespread \nfog early Wednesday morning, and some could be dense. Still looks \nlike the best chance will be across the north where winds are most \nlikely to be calm. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The chance of showers will increase and maximize \nwith the passage of a cold front on Fri, with well above average pre-\nfrontal temperatures that will trend to slightly above average post-\nfrontal. \n\nMid/upr-level height tendency will trend toward neutral to weakly \nrising over the Carolinas on Thu, between a shortwave trough \nforecast to rapidly lift and deamplify through the lwr Great Lakes \nand a \"kicker\" one forecast to progress across KS/OK and the mid MS \nValley. The latter feature will move ewd and across the OH Valley on \nFri, then lift through the cntl Appalachians and Middle Atlantic Fri \nnight. Related modest, 40-60 meter 500 mb height falls will \noverspread cntl NC on Fri, as the strongest height falls and forcing \nfor ascent otherwise pass to the northwest and along the track of \nthe lifting shortwave trough. \n\nAt the surface, a cold front will approach from the west and slow or \nstall over the southern Appalachians late Thursday through early \nFriday, then move east and across the Middle Atlantic and Carolinas \nlater Friday and early Friday night. Weak high pressure will follow \nand build slowly from the MS Valley to the Southeast through the \nweekend and into early next week. While surface through ~850 mb \nconfluence and theta-e advection will result over the srn Middle \nAtlantic ahead of the slow-moving front on Thu, stronger and deeper \nlift will likely not arrive until late Thu night through Fri. \n\nWhile uncertainty remains regarding rainfall amounts, quarter to \nhalf inch amounts appear reasonable given a consistent signal for \nsuch across the 50th percentile of both the AI and phsyics-based \nensembles. For perspective of possible ranges in QPF, their 10th and \n90th percentiles range from 0.10-0.25\" to 1-1.5\". \n\nAlthough the aforementioned theta-e advection will contribute to the \ntransport of ~lwr-mid 60s F surface dewpoints across cntl NC ahead \nof the front, mid-level lapse rates are not forecast to steepen much \nbeyond moist neutral, generally to between 5.5 to 6 C/km. When \ncombined with widespread, multi-layered overcast that will likely be \nin place on Fri, diabatic heating and diurnal destabilization should \nbe limited to less than 500 J/kg of MLCAPE. The threat of thunder \nconsequently should be low, as is typically the case this time of \nyear. \n\nIt will otherwise be unseasonably mild-warm ahead of the front, with \ntemperatures of 15-20F above average and with additional 80 degree \ndays probable at RDU and FAY, then trending to ~5F above average \nthrough the weekend and into early next week.",
